    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mutations of p53 in over half of all human cancers indicate p53’s critical role as a tumor suppressor. In response to cellular stress, p53 transcriptionally activates a set of target genes to induce cell-cycle arrest and apoptosis. While most known p53 target genes are protein-coding, we have recently discovered that p53 activates transcription of the noncoding RNA Neat1. Neat1 plays a structural role in the formation of paraspeckles, nuclear compartments that regulate gene expression via nuclear retention of mRNA. The role of paraspeckles in cellular functions is not fully understood, and the potential role of Neat1 in tumor suppression mechanisms has not been studied previously. This project illuminates the roles of Neat1 in p53-mediated cell-cycle arrest, apoptosis and senescence. Our data suggest that Neat1 is not necessary for p53-mediated cell-cycle arrest or apoptosis as Neat1-/- mouse embryonic fibroblasts (MEFs) retain the capacity to undergo both G1 cell-cycle arrest and apoptosis in response to DNA damage, although apoptosis is delayed in the absence of Neat1. We additionally show that Neat1 is not necessary for p53 function in senescence as Neat1-/- MEFs retain the ability to senesce. Since Neat1-/- MEFs could upregulate compensatory mechanisms to compensate for the lack of Neat1, we further tested Neat1’s function in cell-cycle arrest using Neat1 overexpression. We found that Neat1 is sufficient for p53-mediated cell-cycle arrest, as Neat1 overexpression was able to induce cell-cycle arrest in p53-/- MEFs. Seeming inconsistency between Neat1 overexpression results and Neat1-/- results is likely due to compensation for the absence of Neat1 in the Neat1-/- MEFs or redundancy of Neat1’s role in p53-mediated cell-cycle arrest. Because Neat1 acts downstream of p53 to promote cell-cycle arrest, further understanding of Neat1’s role in p53-mediated cell-cycle arrest, apoptosis and tumor suppression could suggest Neat1 as a novel target for cancer therapies.",
